# Line endings, settled once, for both machines.
#
# This repo is developed on macOS and Windows. Without this file the only thing
# deciding line endings is each machine's core.autocrlf — a per-clone setting —
# so whether a file enters a commit as LF or CRLF depends on who committed it.
# That is how 176 files came to be stored with CRLF while everything else is LF.
#
# text=auto has Git normalise text to LF in the repository and convert back to
# the platform's convention on checkout. The repository becomes LF-only no
# matter who commits. On Windows the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings are
# just Git narrating that checkout conversion; they are not a problem.
#
# Deliberately NOT listing vendored trees (ext/, node_modules/) as -text. That
# looks right — "don't touch what isn't ours" — but with core.autocrlf=true the
# working copy has ALREADY been converted to CRLF on checkout, so -text means
# "store these CRLFs", and the next commit touching one would write CRLF into
# the repo. It also makes every such file read as modified the moment this file
# lands. Letting text=auto cover everything is both simpler and stable.
* text=auto

# ── Binaries ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# text=auto already detects these by their NUL bytes; listing them is belt and
# braces and documents what is binary for anyone reading a diff. A mistake here
# would corrupt a texture or a model, not just produce a noisy diff.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
*.jar   binary
*.dmg   binary
*.nbt   binary
*.mca   binary
*.mcc   binary
*.spv   binary
*.lib   binary
*.a     binary
*.dll   binary
*.dylib binary
*.exe   binary
*.pdb   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.wav   binary

# ── Shell scripts must stay LF ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# tools/*.sh runs on macOS. A CRLF shebang makes the kernel look for an
# interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r", which fails as "bad interpreter".
*.sh    text eol=lf
